{
  "behaviorChanges": [
    "Per-PR ci-python runs one Python leg (3.12). The declared 3.10 floor moves to the new ci-python-full workflow, which runs the full 3.10+3.12 matrix with the same blocking coverage ratchet on every merge into the integration branch, plus a daily 09:00 UTC schedule and workflow_dispatch.",
    "The PUSH trigger is load-bearing, not the schedule: GitHub runs `schedule` only from the repository DEFAULT branch. An adopter whose integration branch is not the default branch gets NO scheduled runs, so a schedule-only full matrix would be dark and would look, from the repo, exactly like coverage. A scheduled run is also redirected at the integration branch, or it would test the default branch instead.",
    "ci-health-check prints platform_required_checks: armed | absent | unavailable | unverifiable, and when nothing is armed it says plainly that CI is advisory on that branch. REPORT-ONLY: it never changes ci-health-check's exit code, because a lane cannot fix whether GitHub is able to gate a merge.",
    "`unavailable` (the platform refuses -- branch protection is paid for private repos) is reported distinctly from `absent` (nobody configured it), and a probe that could not run reports `unverifiable` rather than claiming there is no gate. A bare 404 counts as could-not-run: a token without access, a wrong slug and a deleted branch all read the same, so only GitHub's explicit `Branch not protected` is evidence.",
    "BOTH ways of arming the gate are consulted -- legacy branch protection AND repository rulesets. A ruleset-protected branch 404s on the protection endpoint, so a probe that read only that endpoint would tell an operator whose gate is armed that CI is advisory."
  ],
